+This method samples the secondary boundary integral by first sampling a surface point using a proxy surface (e.g., a bounding box face) and then a tangent direction [S1]. It sets itself apart from [Primary Edge Sampling](7168-primary-edge-sampling.md) by transforming the integral over the implicit surface $B$ to an integral over the proxy face $F$ using Jacobians $J_{T1}$ and $J_{T2}$ [S1]. It is chosen when secondary boundary integrals need to be evaluated for implicit surfaces [S1]. The procedure defines a proxy face $F$, casts a ray from $\mathbf{q} \in F$ along $\mathbf{n}_F$ toward $B$, and records intersections $T_1(\mathbf{q})$ and $T_2(\mathbf{q})$ [S1]. The integral transforms as $\int_B h(\mathbf{p}) d\mathbf{p} = \int_F \sum_{i=1}^2 h(T_i(\mathbf{q})) J_{T_i}(\mathbf{q}) d\mathbf{q}$ [S1]. In general, there may be more than two intersections, and the count may vary per point; the method sums over all intersections $i$, and points with fewer than $i$ intersections contribute zero via $g_i(\mathbf{q}) = h(T_i(\mathbf{q}))J_{T_i}(\mathbf{q})$ if defined, else $0$ [S1].
 
 ### Primary Edge Sampling for Implicit Surfaces
-This variant handles implicit surfaces defined as zero-level sets $\phi(x, \pi) = 0$, which do not offer a finite set of edges to sample from, requiring additional steps to sample visibility boundaries [S2, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from [Primary Edge Sampling](7168-primary-edge-sampling.md) by explicitly detailing the use of AABBs and range analysis for implicit surfaces in the context of differentiable rendering surveys [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Definition:** Sampling the primary boundary curve $S$ for implicit surfaces [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Steps:**
-    1.  Introduce an AABB of $\phi$ [S2, unofficial].
-    2.  Sample points on edge $E$ of the AABB [S2, unofficial].
-    3.  From a sampled point $q$, cast a ray in the face normal direction to find the intersection with $S$ [S2, unofficial].
-    4.  If no intersection, march $q$ perpendicular to the edge and repeat until a point in $S$ is found or the end is reached [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Formula:** Primary boundaries are viewed as the intersected curve $S$ of implicit surface $\phi$ and $\psi(p) = \langle \nabla \phi(p), (p_N - p) \rangle = 0$, describing positions where the surface normal is perpendicular to the viewing direction [S2, unofficial].
+This variant handles implicit surfaces defined as zero-level sets $\phi(x, \pi) = 0$, which do not offer a finite set of edges to sample from, requiring additional steps to sample visibility boundaries [S2, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from [Primary Edge Sampling](7168-primary-edge-sampling.md) by explicitly detailing the use of AABBs and range analysis for implicit surfaces in the context of differentiable rendering surveys [S2, unofficial]. To sample the primary boundary curve $S$, it introduces an AABB of $\phi$, samples points on edge $E$, casts rays in the face normal direction to find intersections with $S$, and marches $\mathbf{q}$ perpendicular to the edge if no intersection is found [S2, unofficial]. Primary boundaries are viewed as the intersected curve $S$ of $\phi$ and $\psi(p) = \langle \nabla \phi(p), (p_N - p) \rangle = 0$, describing positions where the surface normal is perpendicular to the viewing direction [S2, unofficial]. The integral decomposes as $h(p)dA(p) = \sum_{i=1}^{N} \int_{B_i} h(p)dA(p)$, where $N$ is the maximum possible intersections and $B_i$ are domains divided by ray intersections [S2, unofficial].
 
